Architecture requirements

ALVA intentionally centralizes gameplay rules.

Preserve that design.

Relevant areas include at minimum:

* js/game/combat/attacks.js
* js/game/combat/combat-state.js
* js/game/fighters/fighter.js
* js/game/combat/summon.js
* js/game/combat/technique.js
* js/game/combat/deflect.js
* js/data/loadout.js
* js/data/characters.js
* js/game/ai/moveset.js
* js/game/ai/combat-ai.js
* relevant rendering/status code if cooldown-state assumptions change
* fighter definitions and their comments where they currently describe cooldowns

The existing architecture currently has two different cooldown concepts:

1. CombatState.cooldowns for ordinary attack repeat cooldowns.
2. CombatState.abilityCooldowns for summons/techniques.

Ordinary attacks currently allow at most MAX_ATTACK_COOLDOWN = 0.05.

That old 0.05-second design is no longer the desired offensive rule.

Implement the new behavior cleanly rather than layering a collection of special cases over it.

Prefer one clearly named universal constant/policy for the affected 0.5-second rule.

For example, conceptually:

ACTION_REUSE_WAIT = 0.5

The exact name is up to you, but there should be a single authoritative value rather than 0.5 copied throughout character definitions.

Do not use fighter IDs such as 0001 or 0002 in shared systems.

Do not create logic like:

if (fighter.id === '0001') ...

The rule is capability/action based and universal.

⸻

Per-character cooldown data

The new rule must not depend on every character author remembering to write:

cooldown: 0.5

into every attack.

That would make future characters easy to implement incorrectly.

The shared combat/loadout/runtime layer must guarantee the 0.5-second behavior.

Existing character data may be cleaned up to match the new invariant, but character data must not be the only enforcement mechanism.

Likewise, a future author should not be able to silently create an offensive attack with cooldown: 0 and bypass the rule.

Decide on and enforce a clear schema policy.

For offensive attacks/abilities, arbitrary shorter per-character reuse times should no longer defeat the universal rule.

Deflect is the explicit exception and must retain its separate defensive timing.

⸻

Timing and cancellation behavior

Audit all ways an action can end:

* normal completion
* hit-cancel
* jump cancel
* Dash cancel where legal
* interruption from being hit
* paralysis/stun
* landing cancellation
* motion ending early from contact
* summon startup cancellation
* technique interruption
* Combat Assist cancellation
* Void/reset/respawn

The cooldown must not become corrupted, duplicated, skipped or permanently stuck because of one of these paths.

Once an affected action has legitimately started, its 0.5-second reuse timer should behave consistently even if the move later misses, gets blocked, is interrupted, or is cancelled.

A failed attempt that never actually starts an action must NOT consume/start its cooldown.

Examples:

* missing animation -> rejected action -> no new cooldown;
* invalid summon because there is no valid target -> no new cooldown;
* insufficient Energy for a Dash -> no Dash cooldown;
* press while another same action is still cooling -> no restart of the timer;
* successful accepted attack -> cooldown starts;
* successful accepted technique -> cooldown starts;
* successful accepted summon -> cooldown starts;
* successful accepted Dash/air dash -> cooldown starts.

Respawn/reset should continue restoring a clean fighter state, including clearing these cooldowns.

⸻

Different moves remain different actions

The 0.5-second rule is a reuse wait for the affected action, not a blanket 0.5-second stun after pressing any attack.

Do NOT freeze all controls for 0.5 seconds.

The player must still be able to use legal different actions according to ALVA’s existing cancel/follow-up rules.

For example, the new cooldown must not automatically prevent:

* moving/running;
* jumping;
* Shield;
* Deflect;
* a different attack when the existing combat/cancel rules legally allow it.

The cooldown prevents premature reuse of the affected action.

Do not introduce a global inputLocked = true style solution.

⸻

Ground/air variants

Respect ALVA’s actual move identity.

attack1 and midair_attack1, for example, are distinct move definitions selected by grounded state.

Maintain cooldown state in a way that is consistent with the repository’s move codenames and existing attack cooldown handling.

Do not accidentally make the air Deflect share an offensive attack cooldown.

⸻

Buffering

ALVA has an attack buffer (movement.attackBuffer, currently 0.15 s).

Keep buffering functional.

Expected behavior:

* if an attack is pressed while its cooldown has substantial time left, it cannot start early;
* if the press is still legitimately buffered when the cooldown reaches zero, it may start on the first legal fixed step;
* an expired buffer must not be kept indefinitely waiting for a cooldown;
* summons and techniques should preserve their current non-buffered semantics unless there is a compelling architectural reason to change them;
* do not add hidden auto-repeat behavior.

⸻

UI / indicators

Do not perform an unnecessary UI redesign.

The existing visible cooldown-ring system is primarily designed around special abilities.

If the internal cooldown representation changes, make sure existing cooldown indicators do not break or lie.

Do not suddenly put a forest of cooldown rings under every ordinary attack unless the existing design clearly requires it.

Gameplay correctness is the requirement; visual expansion of the cooldown UI is not.

Touch controls, keyboard controls and CPU input must all obey the same gameplay state.

